    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a>, a missionary who wanted to help friars understand and convert the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> elders and former students for decades to record their religion, society, economy and natural world in their own language.</li><li>The work has three texts in one, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, Spanish and pictures, and its twelve books run from the gods to the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> told from the point of view of the conquered.</li><li>Held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and unknown to most scholars until the late eighteenth century, it was translated into English over thirty years and has been put online in full, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> inscribed it in its Memory of the World register in 2015.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> credited by name the Aztec doctors who dictated the sections on anatomy, disease and medicinal plants; he named more than a dozen of them. The Codex was drawn up by about twenty-two artists, a figure deduced from the different ways they drew eyes, profiles and the proportions of the body.</p></aside>
    </section>

